LikeOS network stack host harness
=================================

Copyright (C) 2026 The LikeOS Project

host/test-net.sh compiles the REAL kernel/net sources on the host, twice,
as two independent stack instances, connects them with a simulated wire and
runs tests and benchmarks against them in simulated time.  Everything is
deterministic: the same sources and seed give the same packets, the same
results and the same numbers.

Running
-------

    host/test-net.sh                  # = all: unit + interop + script, epoll
    host/test-net.sh epoll            # kernel/net/eventpoll.c (host/net/epoll)
    host/test-net.sh unit|interop|script [FILTER]
    host/test-net.sh fuzz [N]         # N mutated frames (default 200000)
    host/test-net.sh fuzz --secs 600  # ... or for 10 minutes
    host/test-net.sh bench            # benchmarks, compared to baseline.txt
    host/test-net.sh bench --record   # rewrite host/net/baseline.txt
    host/test-net.sh --build          # build only

Runner options (after the group): --seed N, --hz N (kernel tick rate,
default 200), --pcap DIR (one pcap per test; TESTNET_PCAP_RX=1 records
delivered instead of transmitted frames), -v (kernel log and test trace to
stderr; also TESTNET_VERBOSE=1), --list, --timeout S (per test),
--script FILE (script group: run one script file).

Environment: TESTNET_DIR (build dir, default $TMPDIR/likeos-test-net),
TESTNET_MEM (memory cap of a run, default 1500M), TESTNET_TIMEOUT (wall
clock cap of a run), TESTNET_EXCLUDE (more kernel/net sources to leave out),
TESTNET_REV (build the kernel sources of a git revision instead of the
working tree, e.g. TESTNET_REV=HEAD; the harness itself is always the
working tree's -- this is how baseline.txt was recorded),
TESTNET_RX_BATCH_US=A[,B] (interrupt moderation: instance 0's NIC hands
received frames over in batches of up to A us, instance 1's of B us,
default 0 = one by one; GRO merges only within a batch),
TESTNET_CPU_NS (receive processing costs this much simulated time per
frame, default 0), TESTNET_GRO_OFF=1 (GRO switched off; it is on by
default, as in the kernel).

On this machine, run it boxed: tmp/boxed.sh -m 2000M -- host/test-net.sh ...

Output: one line per test, PASS / FAIL / XFAIL (a known bug, failing as
expected) / XPASS (a known bug's test passes: the bug is fixed, turn the
test into a normal one), and the summary "test-net: N passed, M failed".
Exit status is 0 when nothing FAILed.  Every test runs in its own forked
child with fresh instances, so a crash (ASan/UBSan report, BUG, deadlock)
fails that test only.  A test also fails when the kernel printed a WARNING
or BUG line it did not expect.

How it is built
---------------

* Sources: every .c file under kernel/net (subdirectories included), minus
  dhcp.c dns.c poll.c eventpoll.c syscalls.c unix_socket.c softirq.c
  (EXCLUDE in test-net.sh).  epoll has a test of its own: host/net/epoll
  compiles kernel/net/eventpoll.c with the kernel's wait-queue walker
  against objects with wait queues (ep_kside.c) and checks readiness,
  edge/level/one-shot/exclusive wakeups, nesting limits, removal on the
  last close, thousands of items, fairness and the blocking wait.  A new file the stack grows is picked up automatically.
* Each is compiled freestanding with the kernel's own headers, -O1 and
  ASan + UBSan (the "san" build; the benchmarks use an -O2 build without
  sanitizers, "fast" -- simulated results are identical).  prelude.h is
  force-included: it renames the privileged inlines (spinlocks, interrupt
  flag, rdmsr, %gs reads) and replaces them with host versions that turn
  misuse into a test failure (a spinlock taken twice, a trylock spin that
  never succeeds, sleeping with a lock held or interrupts off, softirq
  re-entry).  BUG()/BUG_ON() abort with a host stack trace.
* kstubs.c is the layer under the stack, per instance: a checked heap
  (kalloc/slab_alloc with double-free detection and fault injection;
  slab calls with interrupts off are counted, NSC_SLAB_IRQOFF), a seeded
  random generator and SipHash, the timer API on the simulated clock at the
  configured frequency, the softirq table with a deterministic pump in place
  of ksoftirqd, one task per instance whose sleeps run the simulation until
  it is woken (blocking socket calls work), poll notifications, user-copy
  and NIC/DHCP/DNS stubs.
* glue.c gives each instance one simulated NIC and exports the instance as
  `struct ns_ops` (api-host.h).
* The objects are linked into one relocatable (ld -r, with --wrap on
  skb_alloc/skb_put so the unused part of every packet buffer is
  ASan-poisoned: the skb pools are static arrays, and without this a read
  past the end of a packet would go unnoticed).  Kernel functions nobody
  provides get generated stubs that abort when called (the build prints a
  NOTE listing them).  Every defined global symbol is then renamed per
  instance with objcopy --redefine-syms: n0_*, n1_*.  So instance 0 and 1
  have separate globals, and the host links both plus the simulator.

When kernel/net changes its internals (new files, split tcp.c, new
softirq vectors) nothing needs doing.  When an entry point glue.c calls
changes (sock_*, net_rx_packet, net_timer_tick, net_init, route_add,
arp_add_entry, icmp_send_echo/icmp_recv_reply, tcp_send_oob,
net_get_stats, net_get_tcp_connections) glue.c follows it.  When the stack
needs a new kernel service, add it to kstubs.c (the NOTE says which).

The simulator (sim.c, sim.h)
----------------------------

One clock in nanoseconds, moved only by the event loop.  Events: frame
deliveries, the timer tick of each instance (net_timer_tick + softirqs at
the kernel's frequency, default 200 Hz), application callbacks.  The stacks
boot at 1 s uptime.

Instance i has 10.0.0.(i+1)/24 and MAC 02:00:00:00:00:0(i+1).  Link 0 carries
instance 0 -> 1, link 1 carries 1 -> 0.  A frame goes: tap -> pcap ->
rewrite hook (NAT & co) -> classify into one of 64 paths -> path loss
(Bernoulli, Gilbert-Elliott, periodic total-loss windows) -> corruption
(one flipped bit) -> path rate/queue -> the link's shared bottleneck (rate,
drop-tail queue, MTU) -> propagation delay, jitter, reordering,
duplication -> delivery (net_rx_packet + softirqs; with rx_batch_us the
receiving NIC collects frames and hands a batch over before the softirqs
run, which is where GRO merges; with TESTNET_CPU_NS an instance busy with
earlier frames takes the next ones later).  A batch is processed at one
instant: the ACKs of one batch, and what the stack sends in reply to
them, share a timestamp, so RACK sees ties (equal send times, decided by
sequence) that a real CPU, which spends time on every frame, rarely
produces -- batch the data receiver only (A), not the sender (B), when
loss recovery is measured.  struct link_cfg /
path_cfg in sim.h; link_stats() counts what happened.

Readiness: the stacks' poll notifications (poll_notify_wq on a socket's
queue) are delivered to an application callback (sim_set_app) after the
event that raised them, never in the middle of kernel code.

Test API (sim.h; stable across waves)
-------------------------------------

  NS[i]->...               the instance (struct ns_ops in api-host.h):
                           socket, set_nonblock, bind, listen, accept,
                           connect, send, recv, sendto, recvfrom, close,
                           shutdown, setsockopt, getsockopt, poll, ioctl,
                           sockname, tcpinfo, tcp_state, send_oob, ping,
                           ping_reply, arp_add, route_add, counter (the
                           stack's MIB counters + harness counters, enum
                           ns_counter), alloc_fail / skb_fail (fault
                           injection), quiet, isn_fix (place the ISN),
                           slab_strict.  Socket calls take the socket index
                           and return a count or -errno (errno values as on
                           the host).
  sim_run_for(dt), sim_run_until_time(t), sim_run_cond(fn, arg, dt),
  sim_step(), sim_at/sim_after(t, fn, arg, a), now_ns()
  link_cfg(dir), link_set_both(&path), link_stats(dir)
  wire_set_tap(fn, ctx), wire_detach(inst), pcap_open/pcap_close
  cap_start(inst, consume), cap_next, cap_next_proto, cap_stop
  tcp_pair(cli, srv, port, &c, &s, &listener, timeout)
  tcp_connect_nb, wait_established, so_error
  struct flow + flow_init + flows_run: any number of one-way pattern
                           streams driven by readiness notifications, with
                           integrity checks; a 10 ms safety scan counts
                           progress nobody was notified about
                           (g_missed_wakeups)
  transfer(a, sa, b, sb, id, n, timeout): one flow
  pat_fill / pat_check:    the data pattern
  frame_csum_bad, frame_fix_csums, inet_csum
  pkt.h:                   build/parse TCP, UDP, ICMP, ARP, IPv4 frames
  CHECK(cond, fmt, ...):   fail the test with a message

A test is a function returning 0 for pass, registered in a table
(t_unit.c, t_interop.c, t_core.c, t_ip.c, t_tcp.c, t_tcp2.c, t_bench.c,
t_fuzz.c) with
optional fields: xfail
(reason: a known bug), hz, ninst (1 = only instance 0 is up), mtu, limit
(simulated time limit), allow_warn, arg.

IPv4, ICMP and UDP (t_ip.c, kt_ip.c)
------------------------------------

t_ip.c drives instance 0 with crafted frames from made-up neighbours
10.0.0.9 and 10.0.0.10: header checks, martians, options (valid and
malformed, with the parameter-problem pointer), source routing,
reassembly (order, duplicates, overlaps, timeout with ICMP time
exceeded, memory pressure, limits), output (DF and identification per
IP_MTU_DISCOVER mode, fragments, EMSGSIZE, path-MTU reports, TTL/TOS),
ICMP (broadcast echo, timestamp, the never-answer rules, rate limits)
and UDP (lookup precedence, bind rules, SO_REUSEPORT, broadcast and
multicast fan-out, receive buffer, MSG_PEEK/TRUNC/DONTWAIT, cmsgs,
checksums, UDP_CORK/MSG_MORE, ICMP errors and IP_RECVERR, raw sockets).
kt_ip.c runs inside the instances (n0_kt_*, n1_kt_*): protocol counters
by name (kt_mib), reassembly state and thresholds, the ICMP rate-limit
reset, path-MTU exceptions, sendmsg/recvmsg with decoded control
messages, raw sockets, AF_UNSPEC connect and group membership.

Regression tests for the fixes of review A (tmp/net-review-a.md):
  udp-cork-memory       pending (corked) datagrams: buffers sized to what
                        was appended, a global cap on what they pin, twice
                        SO_SNDBUF per datagram, a failed append discards it
  pmtu-report-rules     a "fragmentation needed" report changes the path
                        MTU only for a datagram a socket may have sent and
                        that takes reports (UDP lookup, raw bound/connected
                        addresses, IP_PMTUDISC_OMIT/INTERFACE ignore them)
  eth-group-source      a group source address: dropped, counted, no WARN
  out-clone-unshared    IP output never writes into a clone's shared headroom
  ip-unconfigured       an address-less interface takes DHCP replies only
  igmp-mc-list          the device multicast list follows the memberships
  core-xmit-bounded     one send runs the device queue for one quota only
  core-if-events        SIOCSIFADDR/NETMASK/MTU/FLAGS and carrier loss raise
                        their events (neighbours flushed, routes looked up
                        again, a down device sends and reports nothing)
  core-route-oif        output routing restricted to the requested device
  core-pmtu-exception-scope  an exception invalidates only its destination's
                        cached routes, not the FIB generation
  core-recvmmsg-timeout-math the recvmmsg deadline never wraps
  drivers (test_irq_claim)  the shared-line claim of e1000/igb/rtl8139

TCP (t_tcp.c, kt_tcp.c)
-----------------------

t_tcp.c covers the TCP of wave 5: request socks (no full socket before
the handshake completes, their size, SYN-ACK backoff), SYN cookies (a full
SYN queue, the options carried in the timestamp, a SYN flood whose clients
all complete), the accept queue limit, closing a listener with pending
request socks and unaccepted children, TCP_DEFER_ACCEPT, TIME_WAIT socks
(their cost, reuse by connect(), recycling by a newer SYN), the orphan
FIN_WAIT2 timeout, resets on close with unread data and on data after
close, ACK every second segment, delayed-ACK timing, TCP_QUICKACK, PSH on
the last segment of a write only, window updates on read and receiver SWS
avoidance, Nagle and TCP_CORK, buffer auto-tuning at a high
bandwidth-delay product, SO_RCVBUF locking, SYN defaults, TCP_MAXSEG,
TCP_NOTSENT_LOWAT, TCP_USER_TIMEOUT, keepalive probes, out-of-order data
with overlaps and D-SACK, PAWS, RFC 5961 challenge ACKs, pruning under
memory pressure and a thousand concurrent connections (it prints the
memory an idle established connection costs).  Many use a "raw peer": a
made-up host 10.0.0.(20+k) whose segments are built in the test and fed
to instance 0, with everything instance 0 sends captured.  kt_tcp.c runs
inside the instances: one socket's connection state (struct
kt_tcp_sockinfo, kt_tcp.h), the extended TCP counters by name, the
socket, TIME_WAIT and orphan counts, and a red-black tree check.

t_tcp2.c covers the TCP of wave 6: the SACK scoreboard (whole and partial
blocks on a hand-built retransmit queue, the block cache, invalid blocks,
D-SACK below the ACK and inside the second block, reordering, MSS-aligned
splits), SACK reneging, RACK's tolerance of reordering, the tail loss
probe repairing a lost tail without an RTO, PRR's send counts, F-RTO and
Eifel undo of spurious timeouts, the integer cube root, CUBIC's window
over time (K, W_max, concave/convex growth, fast convergence),
HyStart++ ending slow start before the first loss, the pacing rate and
the burst size it allows, ECN (negotiation, CE echo until CWR, ECT on new
data only, one reduction per window), a path-MTU reduction mid-transfer
(re-segmented and resent without an RTO; an out-of-window report
ignored), a GRO-merged segment counted as its segments, the TCP_INFO
layout and values, TCP_CONGESTION, and connect() over a TIME_WAIT tuple
without timestamps (EADDRNOTAVAIL).  Loss recovery and GRO memory:
1 % random loss repaired without a timeout or a spurious resend
(tcp-loss1-no-rto), four losses of one window repaired by one recovery
with one window reduction (tcp-multi-loss-one-recovery), GRO merges of a
batching NIC charged about their size in the receive queue
(tcp-gro-truesize; core-skb-fit checks the fitted buffer classes).
kt_tcp2.c holds the kernel-side probes (n0_kt2_*).

Script mode (t_script.c, scripts/*.pkt)
---------------------------------------

Instance 0 is the stack under test; the script plays the peer 10.0.0.2
(its MAC is pre-entered in the ARP table).  Every TCP segment instance 0
sends is captured and must be matched by a ">" line; anything else it
sends (ARP) is ignored.  Lines:

    # comment
    xfail REASON              header: a known bug
    hz N                      header: tick rate
    tolerance MS              time tolerance of ">" lines (default 10)
    remote_isn N              the peer's ISN (default 1000000)
    remote_port N             the peer's port (default 40000)

    TIME < FLAGS SEQ:END(LEN) [ack N] [win N] [urg N] [csum bad] [<OPTS>]
    TIME > FLAGS SEQ:END(LEN) [ack N] [win N] [<OPTS>]
    TIME CALL ARGS... [= RET]

TIME is seconds from the script start, or +SECONDS after the previous
line.  FLAGS: S F R P U and "." for ACK ("S.", "P.", "F.", "R.").  "<"
injects a segment; its sequence numbers are relative to the peer's ISN,
its ack relative to the stack's ISN (learned from the stack's SYN or
SYN-ACK); payload is generated.  ">" expects the next segment at TIME +-
tolerance; seq relative to the stack's ISN, ack to the peer's; "*" matches
anything (seq, ack, win, option values).  Without <OPTS> the options are
not checked; with them the set must match exactly (nop/eol ignored):
mss N, wscale N, sackOK, TS val V ecr E, sack A:B C:D ... (outbound SACK
blocks in the peer's sequence space, in order).  In "<" lines "ecr echo"
echoes the stack's last TSval; in ">" lines "TS val ~" requires the TSval
to continue the stack's previous one (not backwards, not faster than
1 ms/unit + 50).  An outbound segment that no ">" line expects fails the
script, also within the tolerance after the last line.

Calls: socket NAME stream|dgram, bind NAME PORT, listen NAME [BACKLOG],
accept NAME NEWNAME, connect NAME PORT, send NAME N [oob], recv NAME N
[oob] [peek], atmark NAME (SIOCATMARK), poll NAME (revents of POLLIN,
POLLPRI and POLLOUT),
close NAME, shutdown NAME rd|wr|rdwr, setsockopt NAME nodelay|cork|
keepalive V, setsockopt NAME linger0 0, state NAME TCP_STATE, so_error NAME.
Sockets are nonblocking.  "= RET" compares the return value (number or
-ERRNO name); without it a negative return fails.

Fuzzer
------

t_fuzz.c records a corpus from real traffic towards instance 0, then
injects mutated frames (bit flips, random bytes, interesting header values,
IP header fields, truncation, extension, splicing; checksums repaired half
of the time) while normal traffic and the timers run, and finally checks
the instance still connects, moves data and answers a ping.  A crash
leaves the frame in $TESTNET_DIR/fuzz-crash-SEED-ITER.bin (+ .pcap);
--seed S --iters N reproduces it.

Benchmarks and the baseline
---------------------------

t_bench.c (instance 0 = the client, instance 1 = every server):

  amazon.sN.*  a page load, three seeds (--seed S > 1 loads other pages,
               for averages over many): a 100 KB HTML object, then ~500
               objects of 2 KB .. 500 KB (bounded Pareto, alpha 1.1) from
               33 hosts, at most 6 keep-alive connections per host, each
               host with its own RTT 20-120 ms, loss 0.1-1 %, rate 50-200
               Mbit/s behind a shared 200 Mbit/s access link.  The wire
               NATs every client connection to its own source address,
               as a browser's connections reach many different servers
               (it once kept a per-source SYN rate limit, now removed,
               from shaping the load; kept for comparable numbers).  total_ms (300000 = did not finish in 300 s),
               objects_failed, obj_p50/p95/max_ms (request to last byte),
               retrans, stalls (gaps >= 500 ms in a response),
               max_stall_ms, missed_wakeups.
  live.*       6 Mbit/s for 120 s over 60 ms RTT, 20 Mbit/s, 0.1 % loss and
               a 300 ms total-loss burst every 10 s; a player with a 2 s
               buffer: startup_ms, rebuffers, rebuffer_ms,
               max_rebuffer_ms, max_stall_ms (largest gap between data
               arrivals), delivered_pct, retrans.
  bulk.rttR.lossL.*  100 MB at RTT 10/50/150 ms and loss 0/0.1/1 % over
               1 Gbit/s (cut off after 300 s): goodput_mbps, retrans.

The neighbours' ARP entries are pre-entered (a warm machine).
bench compares every key with host/net/baseline.txt and marks changes of
2 % or more as better/WORSE (higher is better for *_mbps and *_pct, lower
for everything else).  Record a new baseline only deliberately.

Limitations
-----------

* Both ends are this stack: sender-side bugs shape the numbers as much as
  receiver-side ones, while on the target the server is usually another
  system.
* One CPU, no preemption, zero processing time: races between CPUs and
  interrupt/softirq interleavings inside a code path are not exercised;
  lock misuse is caught only as recursion or sleeping in atomic context.
* The descriptor layer (syscalls.c, poll.c, epoll) and AF_UNIX are not
  compiled; sockets are used by index.  DHCP and DNS are stubbed.
* The NIC drivers are not compiled (one simulated NIC per instance).
